10
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:22,720
Okay, so, and the most common measurement

11
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,360
that people use, or economists use,

12
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:26,160
is the coefficient of absolute risk aversion,

13
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:29,160
sometimes abbreviated CARA,

14
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,720
coefficient of absolute risk aversion, right?

15
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,920
And the coefficient of absolute risk aversion

16
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,920
is equal to negative second derivative

17
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:41,760
of the utility function over the first derivative

18
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:43,540
of the utility function.

19
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:44,680
All right, now, at first glance,

20
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:47,460
it is not obvious why that would mean anything,

21
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:49,240
but I will show you some examples

22
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:50,680
that show you it means a lot.

23
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,120
All right, so, let's just start with

24
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,760
this one we've already seen of

25
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:01,600
U is equal to the square root of W,

26
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:02,440
so your utility function's equal

27
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,480
to the square root of your wealth.

28
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,600
All right, so, in that case,

29
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,660
so the coefficient of absolute risk aversion will be minus,

30
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:12,720
and then, let's see, let's do the first derivative first.

31
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:16,360
So remember, this is the same as W to the 0.5.

