354
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:14,000
and there's actually a bunch of models

355
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:14,920
where it's not asymmetric,

356
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:17,760
so it seems obvious to start with a symmetric one.

357
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:20,320
But in any case, here's the deeper reason.

358
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:22,720
It is very standard for textbooks to say

359
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:25,160
that the market outcomes are inefficient

360
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:27,360
if there's imperfect information.

361
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:29,960
It's a very standard claim in textbooks.

362
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:33,920
So there's a list of things, markets work unless,

363
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:36,440
and notice that they do not say it,

364
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:37,960
markets might not work.

365
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:40,200
Normally the phrasing is markets only work

366
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:42,760
as long as we have these conditions,

367
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:45,440
or markets work unless we have these problems,

368
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:46,440
so there's no other way of saying

369
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:48,920
that any one of these problems will mess markets up.

370
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:50,240
All right, and it turns out

371
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:52,680
this is actually a gross overstatement.

372
00:15:52,680 --> 00:15:55,680
So market efficiency and imperfect information

373
00:15:55,680 --> 00:15:57,520
are often totally compatible.

374
00:15:57,520 --> 00:15:59,120
They're often totally compatible

375
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:00,920
contrary to what many textbooks say.

376
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:04,680
And so we're gonna see this particularly clear

377
00:16:04,680 --> 00:16:06,160
when we got symmetric imperfect information,

378
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:08,440
so this is when everyone's equally in the dark.

379
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:11,000
So let me just give you some examples.

380
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:11,840
Suppose that I don't know

381
00:16:11,840 --> 00:16:14,080
how much I'm gonna enjoy my consumption model.

382
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:17,400
So my utility is going to be x to the a

383
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:20,000
times y to the one minus a plus epsilon,

384
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,600
where epsilon is just a random variable,

385
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:26,360
zero mean, standard deviation, or a variance sigma squared.

386
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:28,960
So what is my optimal decision?

387
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:31,520
Despite the imperfect information,

388
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:34,320
my optimal decision is still completely clear,

389
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:39,320
and that is to spend a on a share of a times my,

390
00:16:39,960 --> 00:16:43,000
or spend a times my income on x,

391
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,080
and one minus a times my income on y.

392
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:48,440
Because the uncertainty,

393
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:50,480
although it does make my utility higher or lower,

394
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:53,680
it is in no way even relevant to my behavior.

395
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:55,320
So you do whatever's optimal,

396
00:16:55,320 --> 00:16:56,960
and then you just get a good day or a bad day

397
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:58,360
on top of that.

398
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:01,560
So that would be a case of symmetric imperfect information.

399
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:04,720
And in that case, market behavior's totally optimal.

400
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:09,560
Or imagine that you don't actually know your own tastes.

401
00:17:09,560 --> 00:17:12,120
Suppose you don't know your own tastes.

402
00:17:12,120 --> 00:17:16,480
So it's possible that the coefficient

403
